STDARG(3)                      P32W Unix                       STDARG(3)

NAME
       stdarg, va_start, va_arg, va_end, va_copy - variable arguments

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdarg.h>

       va_list ap;
       va_start(ap, last);
       type va_arg(ap, type);
       va_end(ap);
       va_copy(dest, src);

DESCRIPTION
       A function declared with ... after its last named parameter,
       as int f(const char *fmt, ...), steps through the unnamed
       arguments with these macros. va_start(ap, last) points ap at
       the argument after last, the last named parameter. Each
       va_arg(ap, type) then yields the next argument, as a type,
       and moves on. va_end(ap) ends the walk (here it does
       nothing). va_copy(dest, src) copies a position, so the
       arguments can be walked twice.

       On P32W a variadic function receives every argument, named or
       not, on the stack, 4 bytes each, in order; a va_list is a
       char * that walks them. So:

       -  type must be a 4-byte type: int, unsigned, long, or a
          pointer. A char or short argument arrives as an int: use
          va_arg(ap, int). Structures cannot be passed at all.

       -  The function's prototype must be in scope wherever it is
          called, or the caller passes the first four arguments in
          registers and the function reads the wrong words. cc
          refuses an undeclared call to the library's variadic
          functions (printf and its family, open, execl, execlp).

       -  The function cannot know how many arguments it got: the
          format, a count or a final null pointer must say.

EXAMPLES
       int sum(int n, ...)         /* sum(3, 1, 2, 3) is 6 */
       {
           va_list ap;
           int s = 0;
           va_start(ap, n);
           while (n-- > 0)
               s += va_arg(ap, int);
           va_end(ap);
           return s;
       }

SEE ALSO
       printf(3), vprintf(3), cc(1)

BUGS
       There is no floating point, so no va_arg(ap, double), and no
       long long. The macros do not check anything.
